    "evidence_text": "model with larger unlabeled corpora.\n\nWe discuss these aspects in detail below.\n\nFigure 1 depicts this scenario pictorially.\n\n(4) Add: inserting a new character internally in a word.\n\nHigher order attacks can be performed in a similar manner.\n\nIn the all attack setting, we mix all attacks by randomly choosing one for each word.\n\nThis most closely resembles a real world attack setting.\n\nWe found ATD to be the best freely-available corrector.\n\nuse the entire vocabulary of 78470 words when we backoff to the background model.\n\nWe observe additional gains by using background models as a backoff alternative, because of its lower word error rate (WER), especially,\n\nunder the swap and drop attacks.\n\nWhile the neutral backoff model is effective on 2-char attacks, it hurts performance in the no attack setting, since it incorrectly\n\nmodifies certain correctly spelled entity names.\n\nTwo corrections are considered unique if they are mapped differently by the downstream classifier.\n\nThe neutral backoff variant has the lowest sensitivity (Table 5 ).\n\nHowever, for word-only\n\nIdeally, a preferred defense is one with low sensitivity and word error rate.\n\nWe show that minimally-doctored attacks can bring down accuracy of classifiers to random guessing.",
